<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<model version="NetLogo 7.0.4" snapToGrid="true">
  <code><![CDATA[; =============================================================================
; Pedestrians.nlogox -- hallway scenario
; =============================================================================
; Hallway scenario: open arrivals or a fixed population with periodic ends.
; The shared pedestrian model lives in core/pedestrians.nls.
; Interface variables: spawn-rate, dt, mean-desired-speed, desired-speed-sd,
; noise-strength, simulation-seed, show-trails?, periodic?, population,
; relative-anticipation?, hall-width, run-checks?.
;
; Two modes, chosen by the periodic? switch at setup:
;   open      pedestrians arrive at random at either end (Poisson process,
;             spawn-rate per second in total), walk to a random point on the
;             opposite end and are removed when they cross its exit plane.
;   periodic  a fixed population of `population` pedestrians, half walking
;             each way; the left and right ends are joined, so whoever walks
;             out on one side re-enters on the other. Used for the lane
;             formation experiment (Figure 3 of Helbing & Molnar), where the
;             density must stay constant.
;
; Geometry (metres): a 50 m long hallway, hall-width wide, with walls at
; y = hall-bottom (1.5) and y = hall-top. Exit planes at x = 1 and x = 49.

__includes [ "core/pedestrians.nls" "core/contact.nls" "core/checks.nls" "core/stats.nls" ]

; Build the hallway, reset all modules and (periodic mode) place the population.
to setup
  clear-all
  ; Shared-module initialisation: constants, checks and statistics.
  ped-init-constants
  checks-reset
  stats-reset

  set hall-bottom 1.5
  set hall-top hall-bottom + hall-width ; wall-to-wall width, 10 m by default
  set hall-length 50
  set open-left 1
  set open-right hall-length - 1
  ; One patch = 1 m. Leave a 1.5 m grey margin above the top wall.
  resize-world 0 hall-length 0 (ceiling (hall-top + 1.5))
  ; NetLogo's own world wrapping stays off; periodicity is handled by the
  ; model (periodic-delta-x and finalize-next-state) with period hall-length.
  set-topology false false
  set active-periodic? periodic? ; boundary mode changes take effect on setup

  ; Every wall is [x1 y1 x2 y2]. The end boundary mode is selected on setup.
  ; Grey patches end at y=1.5 and begin at y=11.5.
  set wall-segments (list (list 0 hall-bottom hall-length hall-bottom)
                           (list 0 hall-top hall-length hall-top))
  ; Colours: white walkway, dark grey outside the walls, light green exit
  ; columns (open mode only).
  ask patches [
    set pcolor white
    if pycor < hall-bottom or pycor > hall-top [set pcolor gray - 2]
    if not active-periodic? and (pxcor = open-left or pxcor = open-right) and
      pycor > hall-bottom and pycor < hall-top
      [set pcolor green + 3]
  ]

  ; Seed initial placement and, in open mode, later arrivals and destinations.
  random-seed simulation-seed
  if active-periodic? [setup-periodic-population]
  reset-ticks
end

; One integration step of length dt: spawn arrivals (open mode), move
; everyone, then update the optional correctness checks.
to go
  ped-check-parameters
  if spawn-rate < 0 [error "spawn-rate must not be negative."]

  ; spawn-rate is TOTAL attempts per simulated second across both entrances.
  ; Multiplication by dt keeps the arrival rate independent of the step size.
  ; An empty hallway keeps ticking so future arrivals can still occur.
  if not active-periodic? [
    repeat random-poisson (spawn-rate * dt) [spawn-pedestrian]
  ]
  ped-step
  if run-checks? [checks-update]
end

; Hook required by core/pedestrians.nls (pedestrian context, buffered state).
; Decides from next-x whether the pedestrian leaves the hallway this step.
to scenario-exit-check
  ; The whole open end is an exit. A pedestrian need not hit the exact goal disk.
  ; Keep the fixed random goal as the steering target, but remove on crossing
  ; its exit plane before attempting an out-of-world setxy.
  if (goal-x = open-right and next-x >= open-right) or
     (goal-x = open-left and next-x <= open-left)
    [set reached-goal? true]
  ; Being pushed back through the entrance is not a successful arrival.
  set returned? ((goal-x = open-right and next-x < open-left) or
                 (goal-x = open-left and next-x > open-right))
end

; Open mode: try to create one pedestrian at a random end.
to spawn-pedestrian
  if active-periodic? [stop]   ; no births during a periodic run
  ; Choose an end with equal probability, then a uniform position across its width.
  let entrance-x ifelse-value (random 2 = 0) [open-left] [open-right]
  let entrance-y 0
  let placed? false
  let attempts 0
  ; Up to 20 random positions across the entrance; each must keep the
  ; required clearance from everyone already there.
  while [not placed? and attempts < 20] [
    set entrance-y random-hallway-y
    set placed? ped-clear-at? entrance-x entrance-y
    set attempts attempts + 1
  ]
  ; Skip a blocked arrival rather than create pedestrians on top of each other.
  if not placed? [
    set blocked-spawn-count blocked-spawn-count + 1
    stop
  ]

  ; Goal: the opposite exit plane (hall-length - 1 or 1) at a new random y.
  ; Direction +1 when entering on the left, -1 when entering on the right.
  create-pedestrians 1 [
    initialize-pedestrian entrance-x entrance-y (hall-length - entrance-x)
      random-hallway-y (ifelse-value (entrance-x = open-left) [1] [-1])
  ]
  set spawned-count spawned-count + 1
end

; Periodic mode: place `population` pedestrians at random, non-overlapping
; positions anywhere in the hallway.
to setup-periodic-population
  ; Uniform positions, balanced directions, no prescribed lanes or passing side.
  repeat population [
    let initial-x 0
    let initial-y 0
    let placed? false
    let attempts 0
    while [not placed? and attempts < 2000] [
      set initial-x random-float hall-length
      set initial-y random-hallway-y
      set placed? ped-clear-at? initial-x initial-y
      set attempts attempts + 1
    ]
    if not placed? [error "Could not place the periodic population with clearance. Reduce population."]
    create-pedestrians 1 [
      ; Even who numbers walk right, odd ones walk left: exactly balanced.
      let assigned-direction ifelse-value (who mod 2 = 0) [1] [-1]
      ; The goal is only nominal here: update-desired-direction ignores it
      ; in periodic mode and walks along travel-direction instead.
      initialize-pedestrian initial-x initial-y
        (ifelse-value (assigned-direction = 1) [hall-length] [0])
        initial-y assigned-direction
    ]
    set spawned-count spawned-count + 1
  ]
end

; Random y inside the hallway, at least 0.75 m from either wall.
to-report random-hallway-y
  ; Keep the same 0.75 m initial margin at each wall, for any hallway width.
  report hall-bottom + 0.75 + random-float (hall-top - hall-bottom - 1.5)
end

; Pedestrians per square metre of walkway (0.3 in the Figure 3 experiment).
to-report pedestrian-density
  if hall-length <= 0 or hall-top <= hall-bottom [report 0]
  report count pedestrians / (hall-length * (hall-top - hall-bottom))
end
]]></code>
  <widgets>
    <view x="265" y="10" width="840" height="233" patchSize="16.4" minPxcor="0" maxPxcor="50" minPycor="0" maxPycor="13" wrappingAllowedX="false" wrappingAllowedY="false" frameRate="30" fontSize="12" showTickCounter="true" tickCounterLabel="steps" updateMode="1" />
    <button x="10" y="10" width="75" height="40" display="setup" kind="Observer" forever="false" disableUntilTicks="false">setup</button>
    <button x="90" y="10" width="75" height="40" display="go" kind="Observer" forever="true" disableUntilTicks="true">go</button>
    <button x="170" y="10" width="75" height="40" display="step" kind="Observer" forever="false" disableUntilTicks="true">go</button>
    <slider x="10" y="65" width="235" height="50" display="spawn-rate" variable="spawn-rate" min="0" max="5" step="0.1" default="1" direction="Horizontal" units="attempts/s" />
    <slider x="10" y="125" width="235" height="50" display="dt" variable="dt" min="0.005" max="0.1" step="0.005" default="0.02" direction="Horizontal" units="s" />
    <slider x="10" y="185" width="235" height="50" display="mean-desired-speed" variable="mean-desired-speed" min="0.2" max="2.0" step="0.02" default="1.34" direction="Horizontal" units="m/s" />
    <monitor x="10" y="410" width="115" height="55" display="simulated seconds" precision="2" fontSize="11">elapsed-seconds</monitor>
    <monitor x="130" y="410" width="115" height="55" display="mean speed (m/s)" precision="3" fontSize="11">average-speed</monitor>
    <monitor x="10" y="475" width="115" height="55" display="walking" precision="0" fontSize="11">count pedestrians</monitor>
    <monitor x="130" y="475" width="115" height="55" display="arrived" precision="0" fontSize="11">arrived-count</monitor>
    <note x="275" y="335" width="740" height="80" fontSize="13" markdown="false" backgroundLight="0" backgroundDark="0" textColorLight="-16777216" textColorDark="-1">PERIODIC OR OPEN HALLWAY
Select periodic? and population, then click setup.
Periodic: fixed population, wrapping ends, no new arrivals.
Open: random entries and destinations, controlled by spawn-rate.
The top and bottom walls stay solid in both modes.</note>
    <slider x="10" y="250" width="235" height="50" display="noise-strength" variable="noise-strength" min="0" max="0.2" step="0.005" default="0" direction="Horizontal" units="m/s^2" />
    <slider x="10" y="310" width="235" height="50" display="simulation-seed" variable="simulation-seed" min="0" max="1000" step="1" default="42" direction="Horizontal" units="setup" />
    <switch x="10" y="365" height="35" width="235" on="false" variable="show-trails?" display="show-trails?" />
    <monitor x="10" y="540" width="115" height="55" display="spawned" precision="0" fontSize="11">spawned-count</monitor>
    <monitor x="130" y="540" width="115" height="55" display="blocked arrivals" precision="0" fontSize="11">blocked-spawn-count</monitor>
    <monitor x="275" y="430" width="125" height="55" display="returned at entrance" precision="0" fontSize="11">returned-count</monitor>
    <switch x="420" y="270" width="185" height="40" on="true" variable="periodic?" display="periodic?" />
    <slider x="630" y="270" width="235" height="50" display="population" variable="population" min="2" max="400" step="2" default="150" direction="Horizontal" units="people" />
    <monitor x="275" y="270" width="130" height="55" display="active mode" precision="0" fontSize="11">ifelse-value (active-periodic? = true) ["periodic"] ["open"]</monitor>
    <monitor x="410" y="430" width="125" height="55" display="seam crossings" precision="0" fontSize="11">wrap-count</monitor>
    <monitor x="545" y="430" width="160" height="55" display="density (people/m^2)" precision="3" fontSize="11">pedestrian-density</monitor>
    <slider x="875" y="270" width="230" height="50" variable="desired-speed-sd" display="desired-speed-sd" min="0" max="0.8" step="0.01" default="0.26" direction="Horizontal" units="m/s" />
    <monitor x="715" y="430" width="170" height="55" display="mean desired (m/s)" precision="3" fontSize="11">average-desired-speed</monitor>
    <monitor x="895" y="430" width="170" height="55" display="desired SD (m/s)" precision="3" fontSize="11">desired-speed-spread</monitor>
    <switch x="275" y="495" width="240" height="40" on="true" variable="relative-anticipation?" display="relative-anticipation?" />
    <monitor x="530" y="495" width="180" height="55" display="active prediction" precision="0" fontSize="11">ifelse-value (active-relative-anticipation? = true) ["relative velocity"] ["original 1995"]</monitor>
    <note x="725" y="495" width="380" height="80" fontSize="12" markdown="false" textColorLight="-16777216" textColorDark="-1" backgroundLight="0" backgroundDark="0">ANTICIPATION (applies on setup)
On: relative actual velocity (default).
Off: original 1995 desired-direction prediction.
Other force parameters are unchanged.</note>
    <slider x="10" y="605" width="235" height="50" display="hall-width" variable="hall-width" min="2" max="30" step="1" default="10" direction="Horizontal" units="m" />
    <switch x="10" y="665" width="235" height="40" on="false" variable="run-checks?" display="run-checks?" />
    <monitor x="275" y="600" width="125" height="55" display="lanes" precision="0" fontSize="11">lane-count</monitor>
    <monitor x="410" y="600" width="125" height="55" display="overlapping pairs" precision="0" fontSize="11">overlap-pair-count</monitor>
    <monitor x="545" y="600" width="160" height="55" display="pass-throughs (checks)" precision="0" fontSize="11">pass-through-events</monitor>
    <monitor x="715" y="600" width="170" height="55" display="wall penetrations" precision="0" fontSize="11">wall-penetration-count</monitor>
    <monitor x="895" y="600" width="170" height="55" display="min separation seen" precision="3" fontSize="11">ifelse-value (worst-min-separation &gt; 1e8) ["-"] [worst-min-separation]</monitor>
    <plot x="275" y="665" width="410" height="200" display="speed (m/s): mean +/- sd" xAxis="simulated seconds" yAxis="m/s" xMin="0.0" xMax="10.0" yMin="0.0" yMax="1.5" autoPlotX="true" autoPlotY="true" legend="true">
      <setup></setup>
      <update>plot-mean-sd speeds</update>
      <pen display="mean" interval="1.0" mode="0" color="-16777216" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update></update>
      </pen>
      <pen display="+sd" interval="1.0" mode="0" color="-7500403" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update></update>
      </pen>
      <pen display="-sd" interval="1.0" mode="0" color="-7500403" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update></update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
    <plot x="695" y="665" width="410" height="200" display="lanes" xAxis="simulated seconds" yAxis="lanes" xMin="0.0" xMax="10.0" yMin="0.0" yMax="1.5" autoPlotX="true" autoPlotY="true" legend="true">
      <setup></setup>
      <update></update>
      <pen display="lanes" interval="1.0" mode="0" color="-955883" legend="true">
        <setup></setup>
        <update>if stats-sample? [plotxy elapsed-seconds lane-count]</update>
      </pen>
    </plot>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## START HERE

Choose periodic? and click setup, then go. Boundary mode, population and relative-anticipation? changes
take effect on setup. The active mode monitor shows the mode actually running.
Click go again to pause, or step to advance one integration step.

RELATIVE ANTICIPATION (default)

relative-anticipation? is on by default. The pair potential now predicts the
relative actual motion of both pedestrians: s_ij = T * (v_j - v_i). It uses
both x and y velocity components. This is the elliptical-specification-II
prediction from Johansson, Helbing & Shukla, Eq. (12):
https://arxiv.org/html/0810.4587#S3.SS3.SSS1

This changes one prediction term; it is not a reproduction of the complete
later model or its calibrated parameters. The exponential potential, analytic
gradient, perception weights, preferred/actual velocity distinction, wall
forces, 2 s horizon and other parameter values are retained.

Turn relative-anticipation? off and click setup to compare the original 1995
prediction s_j = T * |v_j| * e_j. The active prediction monitor shows the
formula actually running. Changing the switch during a run takes effect only
on the next setup, so comparisons use a consistent force law. Setup with the
same seed produces identical initial positions, speeds and directions in
both modes. Both also use a separate hard constraint keeping centres at least
0.5 m apart throughout each step. This geometric rule supplements the paper's
soft forces; their formulas and parameters are unchanged.

PERIODIC MODE (default)

setup places population pedestrians at uniform random positions throughout the
hallway, with half assigned to walk right (orange) and half left (blue). The
even-valued population slider defaults to 150. Initial centres are at least 0.6 m
apart, including across the periodic seam. No lanes or passing side are assigned.
All pedestrians start at rest.

The hallway length is 50 m. Crossing x=50 wraps to x=0; crossing x=0 to the
left wraps to the right end. Overshoot is preserved by modulo arithmetic.
Pedestrians retain y, actual/preferred velocities, noise direction, identity
and walking direction across a wrap. A wrap does not create or delete anyone.
The population stays fixed; spawn-rate is ignored. The seam crossings counter
counts crossings in either direction, including backwards crossings.

Periodic pedestrians have a fixed longitudinal desired direction, [1 0] or
[-1 0], rather than a terminal random point. This lets them continue indefinitely
without steering diagonally back to a fixed y or turning around at the seam.
The goal fields retain initial metadata but are not steering targets in this
mode. Pair forces and placement clearance use the nearest periodic x-image,
with a 50 m period. Merely wrapping the coordinates would not be sufficient.

Density uses the physical wall-to-wall area 50 * 10 = 500 m^2. The default
population of 150 therefore gives exactly 0.3 people/m^2. The top/bottom walls stay solid. NetLogo's built-in wrapping
is disabled: custom wrapping uses exactly 50 m, not the 51-patch view width.
Trails are lifted for seam crossings to avoid drawing a line across the view.

This mode is motivated by Section IV of Helbing & Molnar:
https://arxiv.org/html/cond-mat/9805244#S4
Periodic ends reduce entry disturbances; stable lane formation is an outcome
to investigate, not an imposed arrangement or a guaranteed result. The paper's
illustrated simulations omit fluctuations, so noise-strength now defaults to
0. The optional random force remains available through that slider.

OPEN MODE

Turn periodic? off and click setup. The hallway starts empty. Arrivals are
sampled from Poisson(spawn-rate * dt), where spawn-rate is the TOTAL attempted
arrivals per simulated second, shared by both ends. New pedestrians start at
x=1 or x=49 with equal probability. Their initial y and opposite-end destination
y are sampled independently and uniformly from 2.25 to 10.75 m. Each destination
stays fixed. A chosen entrance gets up to 20 placement attempts for 0.6 m initial
clearance. Blocked arrivals are skipped and counted.

Open-mode pedestrians leave when they reach a 0.3 m destination disk or cross
the opposite exit plane. Those pushed back out of the entrance are counted as
returned, not successful arrivals. Set spawn-rate = 0 to let this mode empty.
Counts satisfy spawned = walking + arrived + returned. Dense open-mode runs
can retain pedestrians interacting near different exit targets even after
arrivals stop; check the walking count rather than assuming the hallway clears.
An earlier 30-by-9 m regression retained two agents after 300 s without
arrivals; that historical result is not a measurement of this larger geometry.
In periodic mode spawned = walking = initial population, with no arrivals or returns.

SHARED CONTROLS

Start with dt = 0.02 s, mean-desired-speed = 1.34 m/s,
desired-speed-sd = 0.26 m/s, noise-strength = 0 and simulation-seed = 42.
Each pedestrian samples their own desired-speed ONCE at creation from a normal
distribution with that mean and standard deviation. Nonpositive draws are
resampled; at the defaults this affects an extremely small Gaussian tail.
The positive desired speed stays fixed for that pedestrian, including across
periodic wraps. Both the driving force and speed cap use this individual value.
The mean/SD monitors show the actual finite population, which need not equal
the distribution's configured mean/SD exactly. Set desired-speed-sd = 0 for
identical speeds. Parameter changes affect newly created pedestrians; use setup
to resample a periodic population. The seed controls all randomized initialization and,
in open mode, later arrivals. Repeating setup with the same controls reproduces
the same run. Different dt values need not produce matching open-mode arrival
samples, although the expected rate per simulated second stays the same.

The random-force direction is assigned once per pedestrian and kept across
wraps. It is not fresh jitter each tick. Set noise-strength = 0 to disable it.
dt, noise-strength, show-trails? and open-mode spawn-rate can change during
a run. Speed-distribution controls affect future births without changing
existing pedestrians. periodic?, relative-anticipation?, population and
simulation-seed require setup.
setup clears previous trails and samples a fresh periodic population.

## UNITS AND TIME

One coordinate unit (one patch width) is one metre. Pedestrians use continuous
xcor/ycor positions, not patch-to-patch jumps. All forces act as accelerations.
One tick is one numerical step; elapsed-seconds accumulates dt. Neither depends
on the computer's wall clock. T = anticipation-time is separate from dt.

## EQUATION TO PROCEDURE MAP

1: update-desired-direction computes [ex ey] towards a point in open mode.
In periodic mode it uses the assigned longitudinal direction [1 0] or [-1 0].
2: calculate-acceleration starts with (desired-speed * e - v) / tau, using
the individual pedestrian's sampled desired speed.
3: pair-force constructs q_ij and the selected prediction displacement;
elliptical-force evaluates -grad V. The default relative displacement is
T * (v_j - v_i); original mode uses T * |v_j| * e_j.
Periodic mode uses the minimum-image x displacement before this calculation.
4: wall-force finds the nearest point on each finite wall segment.
5: calculate-acceleration weights each pedestrian force using e dot (-f).
6: calculate-acceleration sums the driving, pedestrian, wall and random-force terms.
7: calculate-next-state integrates preferred velocity [wx wy].
8: calculate-next-state limits actual velocity [vx vy] to 1.3 * desired-speed.
9: calculate-next-state advances position with the NEW actual velocity.
apply-wall-contact adds a solid-wall boundary condition after the free
step: centres stay at least 0.2 m from the top/bottom walls. Motion into a wall
is removed from preferred velocity while retaining its tangential component.
Actual velocity is then recalculated from the speed cap and the along-wall
position is updated with that consistent velocity.

For Eq. 3, let r1 = ||q||, r2 = ||q-s|| and R = r1+r2. Differentiating
with s held fixed gives:

    grad b = R / (4*b) * (q/r1 + (q-s)/r2)
    f = (V0/sigma) * exp(-b/sigma) * grad b

The code uses sqrt(length-squared + epsilon-squared) for r1 and r2 and
a roundoff floor for b. This smooths the undefined foci with epsilon = 1e-6 m.
At exact coincident positions a smoothed pair force can be zero. Pedestrian
pair interactions are softened point interactions, not hard-body collisions.
Initial positions are separated. The independent joint contact pass supplies
the hard 0.5 m pair clearance after this soft-force calculation.

## UPDATE ORDER

In open mode, go first creates this step's random arrivals. Both modes then
update all desired directions, calculate all accelerations, propose all next
states, resolve contacts jointly, then finalize exits and commit all next
states. New arrivals are included in every pass. No
pedestrian sees a neighbour's updated position or velocity during force
calculation. wx/wy stay separate from vx/vy for ordinary free motion. A contact
correction sets w consistently with its corrected v; otherwise both are
retained. The loops use sorted agent lists. setup seeds the generator once;
open-mode go samples arrivals and each new pedestrian's attributes, while
existing pedestrians retain their assigned attributes. Periodic go creates no
new agents and does not resample their directions or force biases.

## PARAMETERS

The interface exposes periodic?, relative-anticipation?, population, spawn-rate,
dt, mean-desired-speed,
desired-speed-sd, noise-strength, simulation-seed and show-trails?. noise-strength is the magnitude of f_i^r
in m/s^2. A uniform
angle from 0 to 360 degrees gives its direction; this choice is not calibrated
to data. It remains constant per person while the strength slider is fixed.
Other parameters are set near the start of setup, in the Code tab:

    tau = 0.5 s
    anticipation-time (T) = 2 s
    potential-strength (V0) = 2.1 m^2/s^2
    sigma = 0.3 m
    view-half-angle (phi) = 100 degrees
    rear-weight (c) = 0.5
    wall-strength (AW) = 50 m/s^2
    wall-range (BW) = 0.2 m

These interaction values match the original paper's simulation parameters.
They have not been recalibrated for the relative-velocity extension.
NetLogo's sin/cos/atan use its degree and heading conventions:
https://docs.netlogo.org/dict/atan

## GEOMETRY AND LIMITATIONS

The example is a 50 m corridor with walls at y=1.5 and y=11.5. The ends
are periodic or open according to the mode selected at setup. wall-segments stores each wall as [x1 y1 x2 y2]; change these coordinates
to change the force geometry. Patch colours only illustrate this corridor;
changing colours alone does not change the walls. In open mode destinations
are independently sampled points at the opposite end, x=1 or x=49, with y
uniformly sampled from 2.25 to 10.75 m. Periodic mode samples initial positions
throughout the hallway and keeps longitudinal walking directions. Neither
mode prescribes lanes or a preferred passing side.

Pedestrian repulsion is soft. core/contact.nls separately enforces 0.5 m
centre clearance along the full step, including periodic seams. This rule
uses a deterministic joint projection of unsafe motion; it can make people
wait or jam. It is not a term in the original paper. pair-contact-enabled?
defaults to true at setup; only disable it for an unconstrained comparison.
The wall-force formula still uses centre-to-wall distance, without a
radius offset. In addition, a solid-wall contact rule prevents centres crossing
y = hall-bottom + body-radius or y = hall-top - body-radius (1.7 and 11.3 m).
This positional constraint is a hallway boundary condition in addition to the
report's force equations. It removes outward normal velocity from both actual
and preferred velocity to avoid continued pressure driving a pedestrian through
the wall. Tangential preferred velocity is retained, and actual velocity is
recomputed using its new norm. Wall contact handles general finite segments
and their endpoints, including simultaneous contacts at corners.

Open ends remove pedestrians at their destination end or on returning out of
the entrance. Periodic ends instead wrap x into [0,50) and count crossings.
Neither case sends an invalid coordinate to NetLogo's setxy primitive.
Compare dt = 0.02 and 0.01 at equal simulated time before trusting quantitative
results; wall constraints do not make large timesteps equally accurate.
Desired speeds vary between individuals and remain fixed after creation.
The 2 s anticipation horizon is independent of the default 0.02 s integrator step.

## SOURCE

Based on equations (1)-(9) from the supplied IKT467 pedestrian report, with
relative-velocity anticipation enabled by default as described above,
including the report's once-assigned random force in Eq. (6). The Poisson
arrival process, uniform start/destination coordinates, entry clearance, force
direction distribution, force magnitude, arrival rule and numerical
regularization, solid-wall constraint, open-end exit handling and the periodic
mode's fixed-population/minimum-image construction are implementation choices.


## SHARED MODULES, CHECKS AND LANES

This hallway model is one of four scenario models. The pedestrian model itself
is in core/pedestrians.nls, the correctness checks in core/checks.nls and the
statistics, plots and logging in core/stats.nls. The other scenarios are in
scenarios/ (Door, TrafficLight, Crossing).

hall-width sets the wall-to-wall width (default 10 m, the original hallway).
Turn run-checks? on to update pass-throughs (pairs that swapped order while
their centres were within one body radius sideways), the smallest separation
seen, wall clearance and speed-cap violations every step. It is off by
default because it slows the simulation.

lanes counts lanes with the project's definition: a pedestrian belongs to a
lane if its distance to the least-squares line through all lane members is
less than 1.5 m. Candidate lanes are split where consecutive y positions of
same-direction pedestrians differ by more than lane-gap = 0.6 m, and groups
of fewer than 5 are not counted. On the periodic walkway the line is
horizontal, because x is not continuous across the seam.

The BehaviorSpace experiment figure-3-lanes repeats Figure 3 of Helbing and
Molnar: density 0.3 per m^2 (population = 15 * hall-width), widths 4 to 20 m,
three seeds, 60 s, lanes sampled every 10 s.
]]></info>
